// @test: typecheck-pass
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: modules, mount, name-resolution, stdlib
//
// Pin (T1006, T1005) — which stdlib modules exist is a question for an
// AUTHORITY, not for a list kept in the compiler; and the spelling of a
// path must not change the answer.
//
// Two symptoms, one cause. Before this fix:
//
//     mount id.snowflake.{SnowflakeError};
//     error<E402>: module `id` not found
//
//     mount core.id.snowflake.{SnowflakeError};      // resolved
//
// `core/id/snowflake.vr` declares `module core.id.snowflake;` and the
// type on line 80. The short form is what `core/` itself and the docs
// use everywhere; the long form is what happened to work.
//
// WHY. `verum_types/src/infer/mod.rs` held `STDLIB_TOPS` — forty-nine
// `core/` directory names, hardcoded in the compiler, against fifty-two
// that exist. Six were missing: `hash`, `id`, `mac`, `random`, `script`,
// `subtle`. A path whose first segment was not on the list was never
// rewritten to `core.<path>`; it went to RELATIVE resolution instead,
// which is meaningless for a stdlib path, and the E402 then reported
// whatever came out of that.
//
// The comment above the list knew the rule it broke — `verum_types`
// forbids hardcoded stdlib knowledge — and called the violation "a
// clarity choice", justified by "every name on this list IS the name of
// a `core/` subdirectory". True, and useless: the claim holds in the
// direction that does not matter. The converse is what the code needs,
// and nothing re-checked it as `core/` grew.
//
// MEASURED before removing the list, so that removing it was a result
// and not a conviction: for each of the 49 names, is the list the ONLY
// authority that knows it — `in_list && !registry && !metadata`? That
// came back false for 48 of them. The 49th, `cog`, produced no reading
// at all, because `cog` is a keyword that resets a path to the cog root
// (T1008) and can never be reached as a path prefix. Redundant on 48,
// unreachable on one.
//
// After removal, each of the 49 was mounted with a deliberately absent
// item: all 49 answer E401 (module found, name absent), none E402. The
// list lost nothing.
//
// The second symptom was that one module had two surfaces — 61 names
// through one route and 24 through another — and the presence of a
// `core.` prefix decided which was consulted. Normalising the bare form
// first makes both spellings take the same route: measured across all 49
// modules, bare and `core.`-prefixed now agree 49 of 49 (T1005).

// A directory the hardcoded list omitted. E402 before this fix.
mount id.snowflake.{SnowflakeError};

// A second one, from a different subtree and a different declaration
// form, because one repaired name is not the rule holding.
mount subtle.constant_time.{constant_time_eq};

// Both spellings of ONE name. If the two ever diverge again, this file
// goes red on the half that broke — which is the property the fix is
// about, not the resolution of any single mount.
mount collections.{List};
mount core.collections.{Map};

// The control: a module that WAS on the list resolved before this fix
// and must keep resolving. If this goes red, the authority the fix now
// asks knows less than the list did, and the two mounts above prove
// nothing.
mount math.elementary.{sin};

fn main() {
    print("ok");
}
